MAIL(1)                        P32W Unix                         MAIL(1)

NAME
       mail - send and read mail

SYNOPSIS
       mail user ...
       mail
       mail -f [file]
       mail -e

DESCRIPTION
       mail user ... sends a message to each user on this machine:
       the standard input, up to its end (^D at a terminal) or a
       line holding only a dot. It is added to the end of the
       user's mailbox, /usr/spool/mail/user, as a line

              From ken Wed Oct 14 12:34:56 2026

       (the sender and the local time), the message, and a blank
       line: the Unix mbox format. A message line that starts with
       From and a space is stored as >From, so that it is not taken
       for the start of a message.

       If a user is unknown (mail: no user name), has no mailbox
       (mail: name has no mailbox), or the delivery fails, the
       others still get the message, it is saved in
       $HOME/dead.letter, and the exit status is 1.

       mail with no arguments reads your mail. It says how many
       messages there are (or No mail.), shows the newest, and
       prompts with ? for a command:

       newline, +, n
              the next (older) message. After the oldest, mail
              quits as with q.
       -      the previous (newer) message.
       p      print the message again.
       d      delete it and go on to the next.
       s [file ...]
              save the message, with its From line, at the end of
              each file ($HOME/mbox if none), delete it and go on.
       w [file ...]
              the same, without the From line (just the text).
       q      quit. The mailbox is written back without the
              deleted messages. The end of the input does the same.
       x      exit, leaving the mailbox as it was.
       !command
              run command with sh(1).
       ?, h   list the commands.

       While mail is shown on a terminal, control characters other
       than tab and newline are shown as ^X.

OPTIONS
       -f [file]
              read the messages in file (by default $HOME/mbox)
              instead of your mailbox, with your own rights only.
       -e     print nothing; exit status 0 if you have mail, 1 if
              not.

EXAMPLES
       $ mail guest
       The meeting is at 3.
       .
       $ mail
       2 messages
       From guest Wed Oct 14 12:40:02 2026
       See you there.

       ? d
       ...
       $ if mail -e; then echo 'you have mail'; fi

FILES
       /usr/spool/mail          the mailboxes: owner root, group
                                mail, mode 775
       /usr/spool/mail/user     user's mailbox: owner user, group
                                mail, mode 660 (made by adduser(8))
       /usr/spool/mail/user.lock
                                held while the mailbox is changed
       /usr/spool/mail/user.tmp the new mailbox, while it is
                                written back
       $HOME/mbox               saved messages
       $HOME/dead.letter        a message that was not delivered
       /bin/mail                set-group-id mail: mode 2755, owner
                                root, group mail

EXIT STATUS
       Sending: 0 if every user got the message, 1 if not, 2 for a
       usage error. Reading: 0, or 1 if the mailbox cannot be read
       or written back. -e: 0 if there is mail, 1 if not.

SEE ALSO
       write(1), login(1), sh(1), adduser(8)

BUGS
       Mail stays on this machine: there is no network and no
       aliases. mail is set-group-id mail, the group that may add
       to everyone's mailbox; it never needs root. It does not
       make another user's missing mailbox (it could not give it
       to them) unless root sends the mail; your own is made when
       you mail yourself. The files of your own (s, w, mbox,
       dead.letter, -f, !) are opened after giving up the group,
       so only with your own rights. A mailbox is locked by
       making user.lock (waiting up to 10 seconds; a lock older
       than 60 seconds is taken as left behind and removed). Two
       mail sessions reading the same mailbox at once can lose
       each other's deletions; mail refuses to write back a
       mailbox that has shrunk meanwhile. There are no message
       numbers, header lists, subjects or replies.
